            <h2>San Diego to Boston Flight Overview</h2>
            <p>The air corridor between San Diego and Boston spans the full width of the continental United States, a roughly 2,580-mile journey from the sun-bleached shores of Southern California to the cobblestone streets of America's oldest city. Nonstop flights from San Diego International Airport to Boston Logan International Airport average about five hours and twenty minutes to five hours and forty minutes eastbound, climbing over the Mojave Desert, the Rockies, and the Great Plains before descending through the layered airspace of New England. It is a route that crosses four time zones, three mountain ranges, and the entire breadth of the American experience - from Pacific surf to Atlantic fog.</p>

            <p>San Diego gives this corridor its distinctive character. The city's military installations - Naval Base San Diego, Marine Corps Air Station Miramar, and the surrounding defense ecosystem - generate a steady flow of service members and contractors rotating through East Coast commands. Its world-class biotech and research institutions, including the Scripps Research Institute and UC San Diego, send scientists to Boston's legendary medical and academic corridor. The city's craft-beer culture, surf heritage, and borderland identity create travelers unlike those from any other origin: laid-back in demeanor but ambitious in purpose, equally comfortable in a wetsuit or a conference room.</p>

            <p>The shift in tempo is immediate. San Diego moves on beach time, where the day's most important decision might be whether the surf is better at Black's Beach or La Jolla Shores, where the weather is so consistently pleasant it becomes invisible. Boston runs on academic and institutional time, where history is not a backdrop but a living document, where millions walk the Freedom Trail, and where the city's most important appointments might be a morning lecture at Harvard, a noon case at Mass General, and an evening game at Fenway Park. For the San Diegan, Boston offers what Southern California, with all its sunshine, cannot: four genuine seasons, architecture that predates the republic, and a civic pride so fierce it borders on religion.</p>
            <h2>Who Flies This Route?</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><b>Military personnel and defense</b> contractors rotating between San Diego's naval installations and Boston-area commands or defense firms</li>
                <li><b>Biotech and pharmaceutical </b>researchers collaborating between San Diego's booming life-sciences cluster and Boston's Route 128 innovation corridor</li>
                <li><b>University students and academics</b> traveling between UC San Diego, San Diego State, and Boston's constellation of Harvard, MIT, Boston University, and Northeastern</li>
                <li><b>Healthcare professionals </b>attending conferences at Boston's renowned hospital systems or San Diego's medical research centers</li>
                <li><b>Sports fans </b>following the Padres on East Coast swings or catching Red Sox, Celtics, Bruins, and Patriots matchups</li>
                <li><b>History and culture travelers</b> exploring Boston's Revolutionary War landmarks, museums, and literary heritage</li>
                <li><b>Fall foliage seekers chasing </b>the legendary colors of New England's autumn from the Berkshires to the White Mountains</li>
                <li><b>Summer vacationers</b> escaping San Diego's June gloom for Cape Cod beaches and Maine lobster shacks</li>
                <li><b>Business travelers </b>in finance, consulting, and technology bridging the two coasts</li>
                <li><b>Wedding and event parties</b> celebrating in Newport mansions, Boston harbor venues, or Nantucket island retreats</li>
            </ul>
