            <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
            <h3>Q1: What is the cheapest time to fly from San Diego to Boston?</h3>
            <p>January is typically the cheapest month, with round-trip fares averaging near $282. Shoulder seasons from late April through early June and September through mid-October also deliver strong value. Fly on Tuesdays or Wednesdays and book at least four weeks ahead for the lowest prices.</p>

            <h3>Q2: Which airlines fly nonstop from San Diego to Boston?</h3>
            <p>Four carriers operate nonstop service: JetBlue Airways (2-3 daily), Alaska Airlines (1-2 daily), Southwest Airlines (1 daily red-eye, launched June 2026), and seasonal nonstops on Delta. American Airlines offers connecting service via Dallas/Fort Worth and Charlotte.</p>

            <h3>Q3: How long is the flight from San Diego to Boston?</h3>
            <p>Nonstop flights from SAN to BOS average five hours and twenty minutes to five hours and forty minutes eastbound. Return flights against prevailing headwinds take roughly six hours and ten minutes. Connecting flights add two to four hours depending on the hub.</p>

            <h3>Q4: Do I need a passport to fly from San Diego to Boston?</h3>
            <p>No. This is a domestic flight within the United States. American citizens need only a REAL ID-compliant driver's license or other TSA-accepted identification. International visitors should carry the passport and visa documentation used to enter the United States.</p>

            <h3>Q5: How much baggage can I bring on a SAN to Boston flight?</h3>
            <p>Most airlines include one carry-on and a personal item, then charge roughly $35 for the first checked bag and $45 for the second in economy. Southwest Airlines includes two free checked bags on every fare. Premium cabins, elite members, and airline credit card holders often receive complimentary checked baggage allowances.</p>

            <h3>Q6: What is the best way to get from Boston Logan Airport to downtown?</h3>
            <p>The Silver Line bus is free from the airport to South Station and connects to the Red Line subway. The Blue Line subway costs $2.40 via the Airport Station and a free shuttle. Taxis and rideshares cost 45 and reach downtown in 15-30 minutes. Water taxis offer a scenic harbor crossing to waterfront hotels.</p>

            <h3>Q7: Can I fly nonstop from San Diego to Boston on a red-eye?</h3>
            <p>Yes. Southwest Airlines launched a daily nonstop red-eye service from SAN to BOS on June 4, 2026. The overnight flight departs late evening and arrives in Boston early the following morning, maximizing your time on both coasts.</p>

            <h3>Q8: How far in advance should I book SAN to Boston flights?</h3>
            <p>Book four to eight weeks ahead for shoulder-season travel and two to three months ahead for Thanksgiving, Christmas, summer peak, and fall foliage season. Data shows booking at least four weeks early saves about 15%. Set price alerts and pounce on sub-$250 round trips.</p>

            <h3>Q9: What happens if my flight to Boston is delayed or cancelled?</h3>
            <p>Contact your airline immediately through its app, where rebooking is fastest. US rules entitle you to a refund if you decline rebooking after significant delays or cancellations. Given potential winter weather disruptions in the Northeast, travel insurance with delay coverage is strongly recommended.</p>

            <h3>Q10: Are there any special considerations when flying from San Diego to Boston?</h3>
            <p>Yes. Boston's weather can change rapidly, especially in winter when nor'easters can ground flights. Pack layers regardless of season - San Diego's consistent 70°F can lull you into underestimating Boston's variable climate. If you are traveling for fall foliage, book accommodations months in advance, as New England's inns and B&Bs fill quickly during peak color weekends.</p>


























